  "summary": "NPR reports that at least 30 localities have deactivated Flock ALPR cameras or canceled contracts since early 2025, driven by concerns about federal immigration access to data and Flock's disclosures about pilot programs with CBP and HSI. The piece profiles decisions in Flagstaff, Santa Cruz, Hillsborough NC, and Staunton, and includes pushback to Flock CEO Garrett Langley's characterization of activists.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The liberal college towns of Flagstaff, Ariz., Cambridge, Mass., Eugene, Ore. and Santa Cruz, Calif., are among a list of at least 30 localities that have either deactivated their Flock cameras or canceled their contracts since the beginning of 2025",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    },
    {
      "quote": "Town officials in Hillsborough, North Carolina announced in October they had ended their relationship with Flock after \"town leaders became concerned about language that could be interpreted as allowing Flock Safety to disclose data to any government entity or third party if the company had a 'good faith belief' of a need to do so.\"",
      "paragraph_idx": 19
    },
    {
      "quote": "In August, Flock admitted to 9News in Denver that it had a pilot program with U.S. Customs and Border Protection",
      "paragraph_idx": 27
    },
    {
      "quote": "On Jan.13, the Santa Cruz city council voted to end their Flock contract, less than two years after it began.",
      "paragraph_idx": 35
    }
  ],
